<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ten Days' Campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ten Days' Campaign was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in August 1831, an attempt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands">King William I of the Netherlands</a> to reverse the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>. Dutch forces defeated the Belgian armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasselt">Hasselt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, but withdrew when a French army entered Belgium to support the new state. The campaign left Belgian independence intact, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> recognised it only in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ten-days-campaign.jpg" alt="Ten Days' Campaign" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unkown, but after a painting of Cornelis Kruseman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_replacement_of_the_injured_horse_of_the_Prince_of_Orange_during_the_battle_of_Boutersem_in_1831_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 had joined the former Austrian Netherlands and Liège to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> as the United Kingdom of the Netherlands. The southern provinces rose in August 1830, and by November, when a cease-fire was agreed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces had been driven from most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Many Belgian conscripts deserted, and much of the Dutch army was overseas fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-war">Java War</a>. William I saw the failure to crush the revolt as a humiliation and hoped a victory would strengthen his hand in negotiations.</p><p>When the National Congress offered the crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a>, William prepared to invade. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> force of 50,000 under the Prince of Orange, the future William II, gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>. Across the border stood just 24,000 Belgians, many of them poorly trained militia of the Garde Civique, divided into the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in Limburg and the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> around the Dutch-held citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, too far apart to support each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> crossed the border near Poppel on 2 August 1831, days after Leopold's coronation, drove the Belgians back at Ravels and routed them at Turnhout the next day. The army then split up, winning at Houthalen and defeating the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasselt">Hasselt</a> on 8 August, the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>'s advance guard near Boutersem on 11 August and the Belgians near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> on 12 August. Leopold appealed for help on 8 August, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent Marshal Étienne Gérard's army across the border without informing the other Great Powers. After the intervention of the British diplomat Robert Adair, a ceasefire was signed on 12 August, and the last Dutch troops were home around 20 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s weakness led the London Conference to impose harsher terms in the Treaty of XXIV Articles, which gave parts of Limburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. When General David Hendrik Chassé bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> from its citadel, Gérard returned on 15 November 1832, and Chassé surrendered on 23 December. Henri Pirenne wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> advance almost resembled a military parade, but that no revival of Orangism followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ten Days' Campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The London Conference of the great powers recognised Belgian independence, and French armies twice protected the new state: against the Dutch <b>Ten Days’ Campaign</b> of August 1831 and at the siege of the citadel of Antwerp in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
